      <article class="prose">
        <p class="lede">
          This month we published <a href={DOI} target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>The AIMM
          Framework for Manufacturing Intelligence</strong></a>, a whitepaper I co-authored with
          Ivo Adan (TU Eindhoven) and Shane Ó Seasnáin (VITO), with contributions from colleagues
          at Brainport Industries, NXP, ITEC, Prodrive Technologies, ASML, Barco, TU Eindhoven,
          JBT Marel and VITO.
        </p>
        <p>
          The framework is being developed by TU Eindhoven and the VITO Sustainable Agentic
          Intelligence Lab (SAIL) together with high-tech companies in Brainport and Flanders, and
          the paper is published jointly by Brainport Industries and the European Supply Chain
          Forum. This article is my summary of it, and the slides above are a talk version you can
          click through or present full screen. The whitepaper itself remains the authoritative
          source.
        </p>

        <h2>The question behind the paper</h2>
        <blockquote>
          What is required for agents to take a larger role in work that today depends on
          experienced people following complex processes, interpreting changing evidence, and
          deciding what to do next?
        </blockquote>
        <p>
          Manufacturers are interested in generative AI agents for good reasons: pressure on
          productivity, skills shortages, growing volumes of production data, and the push to
          improve circularity and energy use. Agents can extend automation to work that needs
          interpretation and judgement — monitoring more of the process and following up on the
          conditions that need attention.
        </p>
        <p>
          But agents are less reliable on sustained tasks. Reported failures include losing track
          of progress, leaving work unfinished and not verifying results, and an error early in a
          task can propagate through every later step. An agent's understanding has to be grounded
          in the equipment, processes and conditions of <em>this</em> factory. The paper doesn't
          prescribe agent designs or model training; it asks how the factory itself should be
          organized so that agents can work reliably.
        </p>

        <h2>Four logical layers</h2>
        <p>AIMM organizes the capabilities needed for human and agentic work into four layers:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Cyber-Physical</strong> — the equipment and processes where events occur, measurements originate and production changes take effect.</li>
          <li><strong>Digitization</strong> — operational records together with the definitions and relationships needed to interpret them.</li>
          <li><strong>Interaction</strong> — the manufacturing services through which operators perform defined tasks, connected to procedures and permissions.</li>
          <li><strong>Operator</strong> — the human and agentic operators, each with a defined role, competence requirement and authority.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The layers constrain one another: what you can observe limits the information you can
          gather, the information limits the services you can offer, and the services limit what
          operators can determine and do.
        </p>
        <p class="try">Click through the layers on <a href="#3" data-slide="3">slide 3</a>.</p>

        <h2>Find the constraining layer before you invest</h2>
        <p>
          For me this is the most practical idea in the paper. If the physical properties a task
          needs can't be measured, the problem starts in the Cyber-Physical layer. If the records
          exist but don't explain themselves, it's the Digitization layer. If the records are
          available and explained but the service can't assemble them for the task, it's the
          Interaction layer. And if all of that is in place but the evidence is still interpreted
          poorly, look at the Operator layer: the agent's competence, its instructions, or how the
          work is divided between people and agents.
        </p>
        <p>
          Much of the capability often already exists — an installed sensor, a historian, an MES
          workflow, a qualification process — so agents can be given more responsibility in
          incremental steps, with investment following the requirements of the work. The paper
          also lists nine questions for specifying an agent's work, from “what decisions will it
          support?” to “when must it stop or seek help?”.
        </p>
        <p class="try">Try the four-question check on <a href="#4" data-slide="4">slide 4</a>, and see all nine questions on <a href="#5" data-slide="5">slide 5</a>.</p>

        <h2>The factory is the baseline</h2>
        <p>
          Factories are built up over years, so machines differ in what they measure and report.
          The paper uses a packaging line — infeed, fill-and-seal and inspection, separated by
          small buffers — to show why physical context matters. A short stop can be absorbed by a
          buffer; a longer one starves the next station. Where a loss occurs decides what it
          costs, so a service that ranks losses by their effect on output needs station-state and
          buffer records, not just stop durations.
        </p>
        <p class="try">Stop a station and resize the buffer on <a href="#6" data-slide="6">slide 6</a> (illustrative rates).</p>

        <h2>Two kinds of context</h2>
        <p>
          The Digitization layer turns scattered records into reusable manufacturing information,
          and AIMM distinguishes two forms of context. <strong>Operational context</strong>
          describes what happened: machine states, counts, logbooks, tickets and inspection
          reports, linked to equipment, orders, materials and time. <strong>Semantic
          context</strong> explains what those records mean and when a comparison is valid.
        </p>
        <p>
          The paper connects this to familiar building blocks: a Unified Namespace for current
          data and events, historians for durable records, the Asset Administration Shell for
          equipment identity and technical data, knowledge graphs for reviewed experience, and data
          products with named owners so the preparation is done once and reused. Change history
          matters too: a production run should be judged against the definition — say, the
          approved rate — that applied at the time. (If you're interested in the UNS side, I wrote
          about it in <a href="/slides/uns/">a separate article</a>.)
        </p>

        <h2>Services: where people and agents meet the factory</h2>
        <p>
          In the Interaction layer, a service — OEE, maintenance, scheduling, quality, energy —
          organizes information and functions around a set of related tasks, so that neither a
          dashboard nor an agent needs its own integration with every source. Services can run in
          Operational, Simulation and Synthetic Data modes, with the same guardrails across all of
          them: permissions, evidence checks, approvals and permitted execution interfaces.
        </p>
        <p>
          Authority is explicit. For a reporting correction, an agent may propose it, a production
          engineer verifies the discrepancy, the reporting-system owner authorizes the change, and
          the reporting service writes it. Permissions are enforced by the service and plant
          systems, independently of the language model's own protections, and safety functions
          remain outside the agent.
        </p>

        <h2>Agents as operators</h2>
        <p>
          The Operator layer treats a generative AI agent as a language model plus its harness:
          memory, tools and protocols, Skills (Markdown procedures with scripts) and shared
          instruction files such as AGENTS.md. None of this guarantees correct execution, so the
          paper puts weight on checking remembered rules against their source, on “loop
          engineering” — when to continue, re-check or stop — and on evaluating agents with the
          tools they will actually use, on cases they haven't seen: resumed work, noisy or
          out-of-order records, and changed definitions.
        </p>

        <h2>An OEE investigation, step by step</h2>
        <p>
          The paper closes with a fictitious investigation on the packaging line. The initial
          report shows OEE of 89.7%. The agent finds an eight-minute changeover wrongly excluded
          from planned time; after the engineer authorizes the correction, OEE is 88.1%. It traces
          a nineteen-minute infeed stop that starved downstream stations, and a technician
          replaces a leaking seal. Days later, it spots an MES interface change that had hidden
          five minutes of stops — Availability falls, Performance rises, and the headline number
          doesn't move. Finally it proposes a suspect–reference–suspect material trial, which is
          deferred when the technician reports an unrecorded sealing adjustment, and later
          supports a material-related effect.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two lessons stand out to me: the headline number can hide misallocated losses, and
          approval is a precursor to execution — not proof that the physical conditions still hold.
        </p>
        <p class="try">Step through the investigation, with OEE recalculated at each step, on <a href="#10" data-slide="10">slide 10</a>.</p>

        <h2>What we don't know yet</h2>
        <p>
          We are explicit that the framework's effectiveness still has to be established through
          industrial trials: whether agents interpret factory evidence reliably, recognize when
          their understanding needs revision and act within production constraints; how much
          supervision that takes; and whether people retain the understanding needed to check the
          agent's reasoning and stay accountable for consequential decisions.
        </p>

        <h2>Read it, and tell us what you think</h2>
        <p>
          The full whitepaper is available on Zenodo:
          <a href={DOI} target="_blank" rel="noopener">doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24160166</a>. My
          thanks to my co-authors and to every contributor for the discussions that shaped it. If
          you're working on agents in manufacturing, I'd like to hear where you see the
          constraints — you can find me on
          <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/nitish-singh/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a>.
        </p>

        <p class="note">
          The slides and this article are my own summary of the whitepaper. The packaging-line
          simulation on slide 6 uses illustrative rates; the investigation on slide 10 follows
          the paper's fictitious example.
        </p>
